$FG,2$Sourcing$FG$

Primary source: Wikisource, which curates only public-domain or
freely-licensed material. Selassie I's writings are public domain
in Ethiopia (life + 50 years, from 1975 — PD since 2025) and in the
United States. The Wikisource translation of the 1936 League of
Nations appeal is explicitly released into the worldwide public
domain. The 1963 UN translation is reproduced under UN Administrative
Instruction ST/AI/189/Add.9/Rev.2.

Source URLs:
  Wikisource author page:
    https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Author:Haile_Selassie
  1936 League of Nations appeal:
    https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Appeal_to_The_League_of_Nations
  1963 UN General Assembly address:
    https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Selassie%27s_Address_to_the_United_Nations

$FG,2$Editorial policy$FG$

Each file holds curated short excerpts with full attribution rather
than full-text reproductions, regardless of public-domain status.
The collection grows by adding sourced excerpts, not by paraphrasing
or by reproducing material without verification of the underlying
source.

$FG,2$Framing$FG$

Selassie I identified as Ethiopian Orthodox and did not claim
divinity. These are presented as the teachings of a head of state
and reformer, held in reverence by Rastafari, not as scripture.
See ::/Adam/Jah/JSNotes.DD.
